Nginx is a high-performance web server, reverse proxy, and load balancer. Its event-driven architecture handles tens of thousands of concurrent connections efficiently.

**Nginx config structure:**

```nginx
# /etc/nginx/nginx.conf — main config
worker_processes auto;  # one worker per CPU core

events {
    worker_connections 1024;  # max connections per worker
}

http {
    include       /etc/nginx/mime.types;
    default_type  application/octet-stream;
    sendfile on;
    keepalive_timeout 65;

    # Include all site configs
    include /etc/nginx/conf.d/*.conf;
}
```

```nginx
# /etc/nginx/conf.d/myapp.conf — virtual host (server block)

# Serve static files
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com www.example.com;

    root /var/www/html;
    index index.html;

    # Cache static assets
    location ~* \.(js|css|png|jpg|ico)$ {
        expires 30d;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable";
    }

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;  # SPA fallback
    }
}
```

```bash
# Test config syntax before reloading
nginx -t

# Reload config (no downtime)
nginx -s reload
# or via systemctl:
systemctl reload nginx

# Check nginx is running and listening
ps aux | grep nginx
ss -tlnp | grep ':80\|:443'

# View access and error logs
tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log
tail -f /var/log/nginx/error.log
```

A reverse proxy sits in front of backend servers. Clients talk to the proxy, not the backends directly. This provides: security (backend IPs hidden), SSL termination, caching, and request routing.

```
Client → Nginx (reverse proxy) → Backend App (port 3000)
                               → Backend API (port 8000)
                               → Microservice (port 5000)
```

```nginx
# Reverse proxy to a backend app
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.example.com;

    location / {
        proxy_pass         http://127.0.0.1:3000;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;

        # Forward client information to backend
        proxy_set_header   Host              $host;
        proxy_set_header   X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

        # WebSocket support
        proxy_set_header   Upgrade    $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header   Connection 'upgrade';

        # Timeouts
        proxy_read_timeout    60s;
        proxy_connect_timeout 10s;
    }

    # Route /api to a different backend
    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000/;
    }
}
```

**SSL termination at the proxy (the standard pattern):**

```nginx
# HTTPS server — decrypt TLS here, forward plain HTTP to backend
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name app.example.com;

    ssl_certificate     /etc/nginx/ssl/app.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/ssl/app.key;
    ssl_protocols       TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers         HIGH:!aNULL:!MD5;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;  # plain HTTP to backend
    }
}

# Redirect HTTP to HTTPS
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.example.com;
    return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}
```

This is what Kubernetes Ingress controllers like `ingress-nginx` do: they run nginx and configure it based on `Ingress` resources.

A load balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple backend instances. This provides horizontal scalability and high availability.

```nginx
# Define an upstream group with multiple backends
upstream backend_pool {
    # Round-robin (default): requests distributed evenly
    server 10.0.0.1:8080;
    server 10.0.0.2:8080;
    server 10.0.0.3:8080;

    # Health check (nginx plus / or use backup)
    server 10.0.0.4:8080 backup;  # only used if others are down
    server 10.0.0.5:8080 down;    # temporarily disabled
}

# Least connections: send to server with fewest active connections
upstream backend_pool {
    least_conn;
    server 10.0.0.1:8080;
    server 10.0.0.2:8080;
}

# IP hash: same client always goes to same backend (session affinity)
upstream backend_pool {
    ip_hash;
    server 10.0.0.1:8080;
    server 10.0.0.2:8080;
}

# Weighted: server 1 gets 3x more traffic than server 2
upstream backend_pool {
    server 10.0.0.1:8080 weight=3;
    server 10.0.0.2:8080 weight=1;
}

# Use the upstream in a server block
server {
    listen 80;
    location / {
        proxy_pass http://backend_pool;
    }
}
```

**Cloud load balancers follow the same concepts:**

```
AWS:    Elastic Load Balancer (ALB = L7, NLB = L4)
GCP:    Cloud Load Balancing
Azure:  Azure Load Balancer / Application Gateway

Kubernetes Service types:
  ClusterIP    — internal cluster LB (default)
  NodePort     — expose on node IP + port
  LoadBalancer — provision cloud LB (AWS ELB, GCP LB)
```

Examine how the sandbox itself uses nginx as a reverse proxy, then write your own config.

```bash norun
# The sandbox frontend is served by nginx which proxies to the backend
# Let's look at the nginx config that's running:

# The nginx.conf is inside the frontend container
docker exec devops-learning-lab-frontend-1 cat /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf
# (Run this from outside the container on your host machine)
# Or look at the file in the repo:
cat /workspace/../nginx.conf 2>/dev/null || echo 'Check frontend/nginx.conf'

# Inside the sandbox, test the reverse proxy behavior:
# The backend API is at port 8000
curl http://localhost:8000/api/lessons | python3 -m json.tool | head -20

# The frontend at port 3000 proxies /api/ to the backend
# (from outside: curl http://localhost:3000/api/lessons)
```

**Write a nginx config for a hypothetical 3-service setup:**

```nginx
# nginx-multi.conf
# Services:
#   web-app:    127.0.0.1:3000  (frontend SPA)
#   api:        127.0.0.1:8000  (backend API)
#   grafana:    127.0.0.1:3001  (monitoring)

upstream web { server 127.0.0.1:3000; }
upstream api  { server 127.0.0.1:8000; }
upstream grafana { server 127.0.0.1:3001; }

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name devops-lab.local;

    # Route /api/ to the backend
    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://api/;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    }

    # Route /grafana/ to grafana
    location /grafana/ {
        proxy_pass http://grafana/;
    }

    # Everything else to the frontend
    location / {
        proxy_pass http://web;
        # SPA fallback
        proxy_intercept_errors on;
        error_page 404 = /index.html;
    }
}
```

```bash norun
# Validate the config syntax:
nginx -t -c /workspace/nginx-multi.conf 2>/dev/null || \
  docker run --rm -v /workspace/nginx-multi.conf:/etc/nginx/conf.d/test.conf nginx nginx -t
```
